    1. Why use Randomization Tests?

    First, detect discontinuities around statistical thresholds. The study needs to confirm whether there is an abnormal clustering 
of test statistics around the traditional statistical significance threshold (such as 1.96 corresponding to the 5% significance level), 
which may be caused by p-hacking (adjusting the analysis strategy to make the results significant) or publication bias (publishing only
significant results).

    Second, minimize hypothesis dependence. The core advantage of this method is that it has very few assumptions and only requires 
that the probability of the test statistic appearing above and below the threshold should be equal in a sufficiently small window. 
If a significant probability difference is observed, it can be attributed to p-hacking or publication bias, rather than the distribution 
characteristics of the data itself.

    2. The principle of Randomization Tests

    The core principle of Randomization Tests is based on continuity assumption and binomial test:

    First, continuity assumption: Assume that for any research method, the true distribution of the z statistic is continuous and infinitely
differentiable (following the framework of Andrews & Kasy, 2019). Therefore, in the absence of p-hacking or publication bias, the z-statistic
distribution near the threshold (such as 1.96±h) should be smooth, without significant probability jumps.

    Second,binomial distribution test. Steps are: Define the window centered on the significance threshold, set the window with half-width h 
(such as h=0.5 corresponds to 1.46-2.46); count the number of tests N in the window, and the number of significant tests k in it; do one-sided p
test (since publication bias usually manifests itself as "biased towards significant results", it is only necessary to test whether the proportion
of significant tests is significantly higher than 50%).

    Core logic
If the observed significant test proportion k/N is much higher than 50%, and the one-sided p value after the binomial distribution test is less than
the critical value (such as 0.05), it indicates that there is a discontinuity caused by p-hacking or publication bias.


    3.Analysis of Randomization Tests Output Chart Results

    Main Results (Taking 5% Significance Threshold as an Example)
    IV and DID Methods: Near the threshold of 1.96, the significant proportion of IV test is 53.9%, and DID is 53.0%, both significantly higher than 50%
(one-sided p values ​​are 0.002 and 0.015, respectively), indicating that these two methods have obvious clustering near the threshold, supporting the existence
of p-hacking or publication bias.
    RCT and RDD Methods: The significant proportion of RCT is 46.7%, and RDD is 47.2%, which is not significantly different from 50% (one-sided p values ​​are both
greater than 0.9), indicating that the test distribution of these two methods is closer to the natural continuous distribution, and the degree of p-hacking is lower.

    *Effect of window width: Even if the window width is reduced (such as h=0.1 or h=0.05), the significant proportions of IV and DID are still significantly higher 
than 50%, while the differences between RCT and RDD are not significant, and the results are robust.
